## 2. Standup — bare bus, attach connectors, load plugins

### [CXC-CBK-STANDUP] Standup is a fixed three-step sequence: bare bus, attach declared connectors, load plugins

- sequence: (1) instantiate the bare kernel with no transport; (2) attach every DECLARED connector — MULTI-ATTACH, so more than one connector may be live at once on the same kernel; (3) load plugins, each respecting its own `requires:` execution floor ([SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s subject).
- multi-attach: a kernel is not limited to one connector. Same-node bridges (`can_mqtt_bridge`) COLLAPSE into multi-attach — a bridge was doing by hand what a kernel with two attached connectors does natively. `segment_bridge` (cross-machine WAN, federating two ComposeBus segments) remains a DISTINCT capability — it crosses a WAN boundary between two separate bus segments, which multi-attach on one kernel does not.
- implementation-locus: LOCKED 2026-08-23 — this sequence's concrete implementation lives in `composeio/composeserver.py`, NOT on `ComposeIOBus` itself. See [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES] (§6) for the bare primitives the kernel gains instead, and [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s [CXC-CS-STANDUP-OWNERSHIP] for the module that owns `standup()`.
- implementation-locus-provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-STANDUP-AND-SERVER-OUTSIDE-KERNEL] (an architect-autonomous Phase-0 build decision under David's 2026-08-23 overnight autonomy grant, recorded on that DEC node for his review).
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the three standup ELEMENTS are David's (line 23309: a bare bus, then attach connectors, then load plugins as "bus services"), and multi-attach is David-endorsed (23389); but the ordered three-step SEQUENCE framing and the same-node-bridge-collapse consequence are the architect's synthesis around those elements, so the node is mostly synthesis and no longer carries a bare david-ruling tag.
- david-quote-context: David gave the three ELEMENTS in line 23309 (a bare bus, attach connectors, plugins as "bus services") and endorsed multi-attach in 23389; those are his. The ordered three-step SEQUENCE framing and the same-node-bridge-collapse consequence are the architect's synthesis around them — which is why the tag is architect-synthesis, not david-ruling.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT], [CXC-CBK-MQTT-INDEPENDENT]

### [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT] A connector is declared config, and CAN multi-attach is blocked on an open wire-format ruling

- shape: a connector is declared, not coded per-service — the same declared-config idiom `transport_registry.py`'s `type:` factory already uses at the Python transport layer, lifted to the kernel's connector layer.
- open-item: CAN multi-attach WAITS on the open wire-format/scope ruling recorded at [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE] (`current-defect`: scope is silently lost on every CAN round trip today, entangled with the [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE] two-layout conflict). This document does not resolve that ruling; it only names the dependency.
- status: proposed
- blocking-dependency: the [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE] CAN wire-format/scope ruling — CAN multi-attach cannot proceed until that ruling lands.
- provenance: architect-synthesis — David only NAMED the connectors (line 23309: "instantiate one or more connectors to the ComposeBus (e.g. MQTT ... CANBUS, LoRa, etc)") and endorsed multi-attach (23389). The declared-config idiom (lifted from transport_registry.py) and the entire CAN-blocked-on-wire-format-ruling dependency are architect-introduced.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-STANDUP], [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]

### [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] A connector forwards by scope and encodes; it never transforms — membrane, not mediator

- rule: a connector's job is bounded to two things — SCOPE (does this cross the membrane, honoring [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]) and ENCODE (the wire format on the far side). It never derives a new fact from what it forwards. This is [CXC-CBK-STANDUP]'s "the kernel supervises, it does not mediate" line recursed down to the connector: a supervisor that also transformed plugin traffic would be a hidden mediator wearing a supervisor's name.
- distinguishes-three-roles: connector (scope + encode) is kept structurally distinct from a state_manager peer (derive raw/semantic input, publish a NEW fact — [DEC-COMPOSEIO-EXTENSIONS-ROADMAP-2026-08] item 3, out of scope here) and from leaf realization (semantic to physical, local, never a rule — [CXC-HAL-ACTUATE]). Collapsing any two of the three into one module is the error this rule forbids.
- realizes: [DX-CONNECTOR-FORWARDS-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] (new design-excellence node, this session)
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT], [CXC-HAL-ACTUATE]

---

## 3. Locality-recursion — one kernel spec, every scale

### [CXC-CBK-LOCALITY-RECURSIVE] One kernel spec is instantiated per locality; a mothercard IS a ComposeServer at the embedded tier

- claim: there is ONE kernel spec. An internal bus is the SAME kernel instantiated at an inner locality — not a second, simpler kernel design for the embedded case. Differences (scope, participant-capability, connector-substrate available) are orthogonal properties of a given instantiation; they never fork the kernel itself.
- equivalence: a mothercard IS a ComposeServer at the embedded tier — same kernel, same standup sequence, same connector contract, running on different silicon with a different connector substrate available to it.
- multi-attach-recurses: an ESP32 acting as an internal kernel may itself multi-attach UART-down plus LoRa (etc.) — the same MULTI-ATTACH property [CXC-CBK-STANDUP] states at the top-level kernel holds recursively at every inner locality.
- participant-and-membrane: every node is participant-up in its PARENT's locality and membrane-down for its CHILDREN's locality, simultaneously. This is [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]'s membrane claim restated as a per-node duality rather than a single boundary.
- root-framing-multi-tenant: ComposeServer's ComposeIOBus is the root of ITS OWN locality, not an absolute root — there is no global root (recursion: every locality's bus is a root below and a membrane above). Multi-tenancy means one ComposeServer hosts N roots. [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY] specifies the N-bus orchestration itself; this property records only that it is the SAME recursive-root claim this node already makes, applied across tenants rather than across scale-tiers.
- root-framing-multi-tenant-provenance: david-ruling — David's `root-framing` property on [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23].
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE], [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL], [CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD]
- multi-attach-recurses-provenance: david-ruling — the multi-attach-recurses sub-claim is David's explicit word (line 23389: "there's no reason that an ESP32's own ComposeBus shouldn't support multi-attach too, e.g. UART mothercard/daughtercard, LoRa"). The rest of the node (the one-kernel-spec/per-locality framing, participant-and-membrane duality) is architect synthesis, per the node's provenance.

---

## 4. Peripheral is a plugin of the local kernel

### [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL] A peripheral is a plugin of the local kernel — subscribe-side, or pub+sub for control

- claim: a peripheral is a plugin of whichever kernel is LOCAL to it — subscribe-side only for pure realization (a display, an LED), or pub+sub for control (a sensor-and-actuator pair, a button with feedback).
- wrapper-locus-is-a-collapse-transport-choice: whether the peripheral's wrapper runs as an RTOS thread in-process over a `LocalTransport`, or as a daughtercard out-of-process over an internal connector, is a COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT decision — [DX-COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT] applied one tier down. It is invariant under the semantic seam: the namespace the peripheral publishes/subscribes does not change with the wrapper locus.
- swappable-realization: realization is swappable behind the namespace with zero upstream change — e.g. TM1637 to CH32V002-raw-LED is a backend swap under the same semantic channel, the [CXC-HAL-ACTUATE] define-once-actuate-many claim applied at the plugin boundary.
- ch32v003-is-a-wrapper: the CH32V003 is one peripheral's wrapper, not a separate architectural tier — "the chip IS the thread."
- esp32-as-daughtercard: an ESP32 configured WiFi-off plus UART-up is a point on a capability spectrum, not a distinct chip class — the same board family can be first-class (own IP, own WiFi) or relayed (WiFi off, UART-slaved), and which it is is a deployment property, not a hardware fact. Mirrors [CXC-BOARD-HW-UART-SLAVE]'s WiFi-off/UART-slave finding at the peripheral-plugin layer.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [DX-COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT], [CXC-HAL-ACTUATE], [CXC-BOARD-HW-UART-SLAVE]

### [CXC-CBK-EMBEDDED-STATE-MANAGER] The leaf may carry an embedded state_manager tier on its inner bus — a bidirectional transform/ruleset

- claim: a transform or ruleset (a `state_profile`) may live ON the embedded ComposeBus at the leaf — a tiny version of state_manager running at the inner locality. It is applied to semantic messages received from the main ComposeBus BEFORE they are emitted on the embedded bus, and it COEXISTS with the pure pass-through peripheral of [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL] rather than replacing it.
- bidirectional: the embedded state_manager is BIDIRECTIONAL — raw → semantic going UP (a raw leaf input derived into a semantic fact for the main bus) and semantic → derived-semantic going DOWN (a semantic message from the main bus transformed before it drives the embedded bus). Both directions are the same tier at the leaf.
- worked-example: the throttle controller — the determination of what thrust % to emit is implemented as a `state_profile` on the internal state_manager: "if the pot value is X to Y, emit thrust 10%" (David, line 23374). The mapping is CONFIG (a state_profile), not code in the connector or in the leaf realization.
- distinct-from-connector: the embedded state_manager is a state_manager PEER, never the connector — [DX-CONNECTOR-FORWARDS-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] keeps transformation OFF the connector, and this node is where the transform legitimately lives instead: on the inner bus, as a state_profile.
- out-of-scope: state_manager's full derive/publish engine and the state_profile grammar are OUT of scope here — a separate, still-undispatched spec ([DEC-COMPOSEIO-EXTENSIONS-ROADMAP-2026-08] item 3). This node records only that the tier CAN live embedded at the leaf and is bidirectional.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David's line 23374: a transform or ruleset applied on semantic messages received from the main ComposeBus before emission on the embedded ComposeBus, "a tiny version of state_manager on the embedded ComposeBus … it should be bi-directional", with the throttle state_profile as the worked example.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL], [DX-CONNECTOR-FORWARDS-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS]

