## 6. Phase 0 — locked: kernel primitives, connector realization, liveness hook, send_message seam, subprocess daughtercard

> This section folds in the 2026-08-23 bus-standup + multi-tenancy design session ([DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]) and its five Phase-0 autonomous build decisions. It makes this spec the correct oracle for the Phase-0 gap-analysis: what the kernel now concretely IS, not only what it was proposed to be.

### [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES] The kernel's bare primitive surface — attach_connector, connectors, set_liveness_oracle, send_message, hook registrars

- claim: Phase 0 locked WHERE the standup sequence and ComposeServer live: NOT on `ComposeIOBus`. A new module `composeio/composeserver.py` holds the canonical three-step `standup()` ([CXC-CBK-STANDUP]) and the multi-tenant ComposeServer (N per-tenant ComposeIOBus objects, the binder that parks/unparks on the requires-floor). The kernel itself gains only bare primitives: `attach_connector`, `connectors`, `set_liveness_oracle`, `send_message`, and the hook registrars (`register_post_publish_hook`, `register_module_hook`).
- why: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL] — ComposeServer is a DISTINCT micro-kernel RUNNING ON the ComposeBus kernel; loading plugins is ComposeServer's job, not the bus's. Keeping the bus ignorant of plugins best-exemplifies micro-kernel minimalism and the [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] grain — a kernel that also loaded and bound plugins would be a hidden mediator wearing a supervisor's name.
- realizes: [CXC-CBK-STANDUP]'s sequence, now with a concrete module home.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0 autonomous build decision; see [DEC-PHASE0-STANDUP-AND-SERVER-OUTSIDE-KERNEL] for review)
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-STANDUP-AND-SERVER-OUTSIDE-KERNEL], the mechanics through which David's subprocess-model and multi-tenancy rulings take concrete shape.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-STANDUP], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]

### [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-MULTIATTACH-IMPL] Multi-attach realized as per-scope-class LISTS; the historical single-valued attributes survive as read/write properties

- claim: the kernel now holds `_local_connectors`/`_global_connectors`/`_remote_connectors` LISTS — multi-attach per scope-class, realizing [CXC-CBK-STANDUP]'s multi-attach claim. The historical single-valued `_local`/`_global`/`_remote` survive as read/write PROPERTIES over those lists (getter = first-or-None, setter = replace the class with `[t]` or `[]`), preserving every external mutator (e.g. `bus._global = t`) byte-for-byte.
- native-multiattach: `attach_connector(transport, scope_class)` is the native multi-attach entry point; `_send_message`/`start`/`stop`/`subscribe` iterate the lists.
- collapses: a same-node MQTT<->CAN bridge collapses into two attached connectors on one kernel — [CXC-CBK-STANDUP]'s same-node-bridge-collapse consequence, now built and behavior-preserving (3903 python + 601 root tests green, unchanged from baseline).
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0 autonomous build decision; see [DEC-PHASE0-MULTIATTACH-VIA-PROPERTIES] for review)
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-MULTIATTACH-VIA-PROPERTIES]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-STANDUP], [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-CONTRACT]

### [CXC-CBK-LIVENESS-ORACLE-HOOK] Node-liveness is a PLUGIN; the kernel consults it only through a sanctioned liveness-oracle hook

- ruling: David ratified — one of four ambiguous-ruling leans, 2026-08-23 — that node-liveness is a PLUGIN (publisher + receiver), never a kernel built-in. The kernel exposes `set_liveness_oracle(fn)`; `node_is_alive` consults that registered function rather than deriving liveness itself.
- distinguishes: freshness classes (multi-namespace aggregation) are ALSO a plugin concern; a single-namespace freshness VERDICT stays kernel — the two are not the same grain (see [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s [CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FRESHNESS-SPLIT] for the full four-way split).
- why: this is [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS]'s no-mediation grain applied to liveness — the kernel exposes a HOOK it consults, it does not compute liveness policy itself.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling — one of the four ambiguous-ruling leans David ratified 2026-08-23 (recorded on [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]'s `ambiguous-rulings` property); the `set_liveness_oracle` hook NAME and signature are architect-synthesis mechanics realizing that ruling.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]

### [CXC-CBK-SEND-MESSAGE-SEAM] The kernel gains ONE generic send_message seam; QoS frame-stamping (critical/ACK) relocates OUT to peer/critical.py

- ruling: one of the four ambiguous-ruling leans David ratified 2026-08-23 — critical/ACK helpers are transport QoS, OUT of bus-core. `bus.publish_critical`/`bus.publish_ack` are DELETED from the kernel and move verbatim into `peer/critical.py` as `stamp_and_send_critical(bus,...)`/`stamp_and_send_ack(bus,...)`.
- seam: the kernel gains exactly one generic public seam, `send_message(envelope)`, which addresses/routes a pre-built `BusMessage`. The QoS layer frames its own envelope and reaches the bus's resolve/notify internals only for the local-landing step. Addressing/routing is legitimate kernel work (the same scope-job grain as [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE]); frame-stamping priority is not.
- why: a kernel that imports a peer QoS module to stamp priority would defeat the relocation and re-open [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS]'s hidden-mediator failure mode from the other direction — the kernel reaching INTO a peer module rather than a plugin reaching into the kernel.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling on the WHAT (critical/ACK is OUT of bus-core, one of the four ambiguous-ruling leans); architect-synthesis on the `send_message` seam mechanics realizing it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES]

### [CXC-CBK-SUBPROCESS-DAUGHTERCARD] A subprocess plugin is a daughtercard of its tenant's ComposeServer over IPC — the mothercard recursion one tier up, applied to software

- ruling: David ruled (subprocess-model, part a): a subprocess plugin lives INSIDE its tenant's ComposeServer locality, reached over an internal IPC connector — it does NOT share the ComposeIOBus object. ComposeServer supervises its lifecycle (spawn/config/restart) but NEVER mediates its bus traffic. Structurally it is a daughtercard of ComposeServer over IPC — the mothercard recursion, one tier up. Its `local` scope stays in the tenant; only `global` uplinks.
- generalizes: [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL]'s `wrapper-locus-is-a-collapse-transport-choice` — whether a peripheral's wrapper runs in-process over a `LocalTransport` or out-of-process over an internal connector was already stated as a collapse-transport decision invariant under the semantic seam. This node is the SAME pattern one tier up: a SOFTWARE plugin's wrapper locus (in-process vs. subprocess-over-IPC) is the identical collapse-transport choice, now made concrete for ComposeServer plugins.
- phase-0-seam: a plugin whose resolved isolation is >= subprocess (David's Q1 HARD pin, [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]'s [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]) is recorded `subprocess_pending` and NOT started in-process; the IPC daughtercard host is Phase 1 — the seam is explicit rather than silently starting it in-process.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling — David's subprocess-model ruling (a) on [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]; the `generalizes` cross-reference to [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL] and the `phase-0-seam` property are architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PERIPHERAL-PLUGIN-MODEL], [CXC-CBK-LOCALITY-RECURSIVE], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]

