## 7. Packaging — the composebus distribution (v0.1.0)


### [CXC-CBK-PKG-DISTRIBUTION] The distribution is `composebus` 0.1.0 at `python/composebus/`, import package `composebus`, depending on pydantic only

- requirement: the composeio repo MUST contain a distribution named `composebus`, version `0.1.0`, rooted at `python/composebus/` with its OWN `pyproject.toml`. Its import package MUST be `composebus`.
- runtime-dependencies: exactly one — `pydantic>=2.0`. No other runtime dependency may be declared (no paho, no yaml, no composeio).
- verify-machine: parse `python/composebus/pyproject.toml`; assert `project.name == "composebus"`, `project.version == "0.1.0"`, and `project.dependencies` equals `["pydantic>=2.0"]` (whitespace-insensitive); assert `python/composebus/composebus/__init__.py` exists.
- verify-human: none required — no human-perceptible surface.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-CONTENTS], [CXC-CBK-PKG-CLOSURE-RULE]
- monorepo-resolution: TRANSITIONAL. `composeio/__init__.py` first tries `import composebus`. On ImportError it inserts the sibling directory `python/composebus` (located relative to `composeio/__file__`, never a hard-coded absolute path) at the front of sys.path and retries. An installed composebus therefore always wins. This keeps every existing entrypoint working unchanged (`cd python && pytest`, `PYTHONPATH=.` services, launchd, the ComposeServer container), and the pre-commit hook must still pass. Removal condition: when composeio declares a dependency on a published composebus release, delete the fallback (follow-on). (Architect ruling, 2026-11-25, on the builder's pre-integration question 2.)
- amendment-ship-and-install-paths: (Architect ruling 2026-11-25, after QA FAIL F1/F2.) (1) EVERY path that ships or installs `python/composeio` must also ship or install `python/composebus`: Dockerfiles, rsync/deploy scripts, build.sh files, launchd/systemd units, READMEs that document an install. Find them WIDE first: every literal `python/composeio`, `composeio/python` and `-e ...python` in the repo, outside `python/composeio` itself. Report the funnel (N matched, M are ship/install paths, K changed), then fix each one. (2) `python/pyproject.toml` declares its packages explicitly (`composeio*` only, so auto-discovery no longer sees `composebus`) and declares a runtime dependency `composebus==0.1.0`. (3) The documented install is one pip command that resolves both locally: `pip install ./python/composebus ./python`, or `-e` for each. Verify each repaired path by SIMULATION OUTSIDE the clone, the way QA did (git archive the shipped subset, run its entrypoint), with the pre-move base as a control.

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-CONTENTS] The package contents are the measured bare-bus import closure plus the envelope and transport contract — and no other module moves

- requirement: the following modules MUST move from `python/composeio/` into `composebus` (same file names): `bus.py`, `bus_clock.py`, `bus_envelope.py`, `freshness.py`, `module.py`, `namespace.py`, `selector.py`, `state_engine.py`, `transport.py`, `transport_contract.py`, `transport_local.py`, `plugin.py`, and the subpackage `plugins/` containing exactly `__init__.py`, `state_manager.py`, `discovery.py`, `time.py`, `liveness.py`, `log.py`.
- basis: the bare-bus top-level import closure, measured 2026-11-25, plus the envelope and transport contract. A bare `ComposeIOBus()` constructor installs those five default plugins (state_manager, discovery, time, liveness, log); `plugin.py` needs pydantic.
- exclusion: NO other module moves. Every other module under `python/composeio/` (including `composeserver.py`, `transport_registry.py`, and `peer/`) stays where it is. A builder who believes another module must move STOPS under [CXC-CBK-PKG-CLOSURE-RULE]; it does not move it.
- verify-machine: a test lists the `.py` files under the installed `composebus` package and asserts the set equals the 12 named top-level modules plus the 6 `plugins/` files; a second assertion confirms no file outside that list was deleted from `python/composeio/`.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-DISTRIBUTION], [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES], [CXC-CBK-MQTT-INDEPENDENT]
- amendment-transforms: ADD `python/composeio/transforms.py` (187 lines, stdlib only) to the move list. Found by the builder's closure pre-scan (STOP raised 2026-11-25 22:36:29 UTC at 07348ff): `plugins/state_manager.py:93` lazily imports `composeio.transforms.TransformChain` on the `ComposeIOBus.add_binding` path, so a bare bus reaches it. Counts become 13 top-level modules plus 6 `plugins/` files. Injecting TransformChain instead is a redesign, so it is out of §7's scope. (Architect ruling, 2026-11-25.)

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-CLOSURE-RULE] Closure rule — nothing in composebus imports outside composebus, the standard library and pydantic, lazy imports included — with an explicit STOP condition

- rule: no module in `composebus` may import anything outside (a) `composebus` itself, (b) the Python standard library, (c) `pydantic` — at module top level OR inside any function, method or class body (lazy imports count).
- measurement: an AST walk over every `.py` file in the package collects every `Import` and `ImportFrom` node at any depth (not only the module body) and classifies each root module name against that allow-list. Relative imports resolve inside `composebus` and are allowed. The violation set MUST be empty.
- stop-condition: if the builder finds an import — top-level or lazy — that reaches outside the allow-list, it STOPS and reports it to the architect as a SPEC QUESTION (module, line, imported name, the call path that reaches it). It MUST NOT move additional modules to satisfy the rule, MUST NOT delete or stub the import, and MUST NOT widen the allow-list. The architect decides; the spec is amended.
- why: the closure is what makes the package pinnable by a consumer that has never seen the monorepo; one lazy `import composeio.x` inside a function body would pass every top-level check and fail at runtime in the consumer.
- verify-machine: the AST-walk script exits 0 with an empty violation set; its raw output is pasted into the closure-check slot of [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE].
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-CONTENTS], [CXC-CBK-PKG-STANDALONE-PROOF], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-COMPAT-IDENTITY] Every moved `composeio.<name>` stays importable and resolves to the IDENTICAL module object

- requirement: for every moved module `<name>` (and `plugins` and each `plugins.<x>`), `import composeio.<name>` MUST succeed, and `composeio.<name> is composebus.<name>` MUST be true (object identity, not equality). Consequence: monkeypatching through either path affects both, and `isinstance` checks across the two names agree.
- mechanism: the builder's choice — `sys.modules` aliasing or a re-export shim. Identity is the acceptance criterion, not the mechanism. A shim that re-exports names but produces a distinct module object FAILS this node.
- boundary: `from composeio.bus import ComposeIOBus` and `from composebus.bus import ComposeIOBus` MUST yield the same class object; a monkeypatch of `composeio.bus.<attr>` MUST be observable as `composebus.bus.<attr>`.
- verify-machine: a test iterates the 12 moved top-level modules plus `plugins` and its 5 submodules, importing each by both names and asserting `is`; plus one monkeypatch round-trip test on `bus`.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-CONTENTS], [CXC-CBK-PKG-BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVED]
- amendment-plugins-package: The PACKAGE `composeio.plugins` is NOT aliased. It stays a real package in the monorepo, because three non-moving plugins live in it (`bus_storage.py`, `fingerprint_manager.py`, `fingerprint_template_store.py`). Identity is required for each of the 5 moved SUBMODULES: `composeio.plugins.<x> is composebus.plugins.<x>` for x in state_manager, discovery, time, liveness, log. `composebus.plugins` is its own package holding only those 5 plus its `__init__`. The non-moving plugins must NOT become importable as `composebus.plugins.*`: no `__path__` grafting. This supersedes this node's line requiring identity for `plugins` itself. (Architect ruling, 2026-11-25, on the builder's pre-integration question 1.)

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVED] Behaviour is preserved — the full existing python test suite has identical pass/fail/skip counts before and after

- requirement: the full existing python test suite MUST report the same pass, fail and skip counts after the change as before it. The pre-change baseline MUST be recorded BEFORE any file moves, in the baseline slot of [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]; the after-counts go in the after slot.
- boundary: a new test added by this change (closure, identity, contents, standalone) is reported SEPARATELY and does not enter the before/after comparison. A previously failing or skipped test stays failing or skipped: this change does not fix or un-skip anything, and a count that improves is as much a discrepancy as one that worsens.
- verify-machine: the three counts after equal the three counts before, field by field.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-COMPAT-IDENTITY], [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-STANDALONE-PROOF] Standalone proof — a kernel-only test subset passes in a fresh venv holding only composebus and pydantic, and `import composeio` FAILS there (negative control)

- requirement: create a FRESH virtualenv; install ONLY `composebus` (from `python/composebus/`) — which brings pydantic — plus the test runner. Run a kernel-only test subset (the existing tests that exercise only moved modules; the builder selects them by the closure measurement and lists them in the evidence slot). The subset MUST pass.
- negative-control: in the SAME venv, `python -c "import composeio"` MUST fail with `ModuleNotFoundError`. This is mandatory: without it a passing subset could be silently importing the monorepo from the working directory or a develop-install. A venv where `import composeio` succeeds INVALIDATES the standalone result, however green the subset is.
- boundary: the venv is run from a directory outside the repository checkout, so the working directory cannot supply `composeio`.
- verify-machine: subset exit code 0 AND the negative-control command exits non-zero with `ModuleNotFoundError` in its output; both captured verbatim in the standalone slot of [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE].
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-CLOSURE-RULE], [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]
- test-import-rule: the selected existing tests import `composeio.*` and so cannot run unchanged in a venv where `import composeio` fails. They are COPIED into `python/composebus/tests/` with ONLY their import lines rewritten from `composeio` to `composebus` (assertions, fixtures and bodies byte-identical). The originals stay where they are and still run in the full suite. Proof: a diff of each copy against its original whose every changed line is an import line, pasted into the standalone slot. (Architect amendment at ratify, 2026-11-25.)

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-RELEASE] Release mechanics — annotated tag `composebus-v0.1.0`, LOCAL ONLY; nothing is pushed

- requirement: after every other §7 predicate passes, create the ANNOTATED git tag `composebus-v0.1.0` on the commit carrying the package. The tag is LOCAL ONLY.
- prohibition: nothing is pushed anywhere — not the tag, not the branch, not to any remote. Pushing needs HoP's word via a later DSD (a decision, not a build step).
- consumer-pin: once pushed (not before), Gnomon pins with `composebus @ git+https://github.com/740Dave/composeio@composebus-v0.1.0#subdirectory=python/composebus`. This string is recorded here so the tag name and package subdirectory are not changed without amending the pin.
- verify-machine: `git cat-file -t composebus-v0.1.0` prints `tag` (annotated, not lightweight); `git ls-remote --tags origin composebus-v0.1.0` returns nothing; the tag's commit SHA goes in the tag slot of [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE].
- verify-human: HoP's word is required for the later push; that word is NOT part of this section's acceptance.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes HoP's public-first direction while withholding the push, which HoP has reserved.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-DISTRIBUTION], [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]
- amendment-licence-and-refnames: (Architect ruling 2026-11-25, after QA FAIL F3 and note 1.) (1) `python/composebus/LICENSE` carries the MIT text, copied verbatim from the public repo's LICENSE. `pyproject.toml` declares the licence in a form its setuptools floor supports, and the built wheel must include the licence file; verify by listing the wheel's contents. (2) No branch may share the tag's name. The build branch is `build/composebus-v0.1.0`, and the pin must resolve to the tagged commit.
- published: PUBLISHED 2026-11-26 by git-expert, under DEC-HOP-STANDING-PUSH-AUTHORITY-COMPOSEIO-2026-11-25 while HoP was away. Public 740Dave/composeio main: d856590..7bad79018c7914ef869a6096bba608d8b21f0ad2, a fast-forward that adds only python/composebus/ (36 files, +7765), extracted from internal 6a14673. Annotated tag composebus-v0.1.0, object eb61cdc5bb289399791e5d4287be93264b123e17, peels to 7bad790. Leak scan before the push: every secret, path, host and org class 0; only permitted classes hit (owner name 1 in LICENSE, ip:bsg 2, bio:fingerprint 13). Verified after the push: ls-remote shows both refs; a fresh venv installed the pin `composebus @ git+https://github.com/740Dave/composeio@composebus-v0.1.0#subdirectory=python/composebus`; `ComposeIOBus()` constructs from outside every clone; `import composeio` fails. Internal 740Dave/composeio-internal main: 07348ff..6a14673, a fast-forward; others' uncommitted work had 0 overlap and was left untouched; the tag was not pushed internally.

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-LEAK-HYGIENE] Leak hygiene — nothing in composebus names a host, private IP, home path or a deployment name (Haven, for example); two BSG docstring mentions and the owner's first name may stay

- permitted: 2 docstring mentions of a BSG term in `namespace.py` and `transport.py`, and the owner's first name. HoP ruled 2026-11-25 that his name and references to his ComposeIO projects need no screening.
- verify-machine: the builder greps every file in the package (including `pyproject.toml` and docstrings) for each forbidden class and records the command and its output in the leak slot of [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]. A hit is reported to the architect; the builder does not rewrite code to make it vanish, because a docstring edit would be a non-moving change outside the packaging scope.
- verify-human: none required.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — HoP's 2026-11-25 ruling that his name and references to his ComposeIO projects need no screening; the forbidden-class list is architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-PKG-CONTENTS], [CXC-CBK-PKG-EVIDENCE]

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-STDLIB-ONLY-FOLLOWON] Follow-on, OUT OF SCOPE — a stdlib-only kernel with default plugins injected by ComposeServer

- proposal: a later change could make the kernel stdlib-only by removing the default-plugin install from the bare-bus constructor and having ComposeServer inject those plugins. That would drop the pydantic dependency from the kernel and follows [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES]'s grain (loading plugins is ComposeServer's job, not the bus's).
- scope-boundary: NOT part of v0.1.0. v0.1.0 preserves the current constructor behaviour exactly ([CXC-CBK-PKG-BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVED]); this node records the direction so it is not lost, and authorizes no work.
- invalidating_assumption: if a consumer of the public package needs a pydantic-free kernel before ComposeServer-side injection is designed, the order of the two changes reopens.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-KERNEL-PRIMITIVES], [CXC-CBK-PKG-DISTRIBUTION]

### [CXC-CBK-PKG-PRE-INTEGRATION] Pre-integration questions — none open

- questions: none. Every packaging choice is ratified above. The one point left to the builder, the aliasing mechanism, is explicitly delegated by [CXC-CBK-PKG-COMPAT-IDENTITY]; the one foreseeable surprise, a lazy import reaching outside the closure, has a prescribed STOP in [CXC-CBK-PKG-CLOSURE-RULE]. A builder who finds a genuine ambiguity records it as a spec question to the architect rather than choosing silently.
